        "type": "slide",
        "before": "3.1 Authentic Learning\nTeaching Channel meets the intent of ISTE 3.1 Authentic Learning by grounding professional learning in real classroom practice and authentic instructional challenges. Our videos are intentionally selected and filmed in real educational settings, with schools and educators chosen based on instructional priorities, content needs, and the value of the practices being demonstrated. Teachers provide lesson plans, instructional materials, and classroom artifacts in advance, allowing the video experience to capture not only what happens in the classroom, but also the instructional thinking and context behind the practice.\nRather than presenting a single “perfect” lesson for educators to copy, Teaching Channel uses exemplar videos as models that encourage inquiry, discussion, reflection, and professional problem solving. Educators can examine authentic classroom scenarios, consider how the strategies address real instructional needs, and then adapt or repurpose the ideas, resources, and approaches for their own students and learning environments. This supports the key elements of ISTE 3.1 by giving educators opportunities to engage with real-world problems, develop context-specific solutions, and apply or remix professional learning resources in meaningful ways.\nContent is developed with educational expertise, including production leadership with advanced credentials in Curriculum and Instruction and Educational Technology, helping ensure each video is instructionally relevant and intentionally designed to support educator growth. ",
