    "revisedNotes": "4.3.a Cultivate Coaching Relationships\n\nScope: Teaching Channel Video Platform. Evidence review dated September 22, 2026.\n\nReviewer walkthrough\nConnect the supplied coaching conversation to a resource, an educator’s strategy trial, and a follow-up reflection. This is a coaching process, not verified conference scheduling functionality.\n\nEvidence basis\nEducator/coach demo; /TeachingChannel/PlaylistsPage; public slide 61 viewer; Controllers/ActivityCenterController.cs:824; official Coaches 4.3.a\n\nBefore submission\nConnect the coaching conversation to a strategy trial and follow-up evidence.\n\nThese notes describe evidence and verification limits; they are not an ISTE approval or a substitute for the official indicator requirements.",
    "originalText": "4.3.a Cultivate Coaching Relationships \nTeaching Channel facilitates the complete coaching cycle by providing a collaborative platform where educators and instructional coaches can monitor and celebrate professional development from the initial pre-conference through the post-conference. This environment enables users to establish clear objectives, examine instructional strategies via video, engage in constructive feedback and reflection, and continuously assess progress to foster transparent professional growth.\nPre-Discussion and Goal Setting The instructional coach and educator engage in collaborative pre-planning focused on enhancing student collaboration strategies. Building upon a two-year mentoring relationship, this process further strengthens the professional partnership between the coach and the educator.",
    "revisedText": "4.3.a Cultivate Coaching Relationships\n\nCoaches and educators can use the platform to share resources, document goals, and discuss classroom practice through video and feedback. These tools can support an ongoing coaching relationship.\nThe supplied pre-discussion example should be connected to a shared resource, an educator’s attempt at a strategy, and a follow-up reflection. Pre- and post-conferences are part of the coaching practice, not a verified scheduling feature.",
